const SILVERSTRIPE_RESEARCH_DIRECTIVE = `

SILVERSTRIPE PROJECT DETECTED. During your research you MUST specifically investigate:

RESEARCH EFFICIENCY (MANDATORY — prevents runaway research phase):
- Do NOT read the same file twice. Keep a mental list of files already read and skip duplicate read_file or execute_bash calls for the same path.
- Do NOT run the same grep command twice. If you've already searched for a pattern, trust the earlier result.
- Stop adding tool calls once you have sufficient evidence for every section of KEY FINDINGS. Research that runs for 30+ minutes burns the session budget before any code is written.
- If a file read reveals nothing relevant to the task, stop reading it — do not paginate to additional sections "just in case".

1. Read composer.json to identify the SilverStripe version (look for silverstripe/framework constraint).
2. List all files under app/_config/ (or mysite/_config/) and READ EVERY ONE OF THEM — not just files that seem relevant. A feature you think is missing may already be configured in a file you haven't read.

ALREADY-IMPLEMENTED CHECK (CRITICAL — prevents infinite retry loops):
After reading all _config files, check whether the task's core feature is already in place:
- If the task asks to add Elemental extensions and you find DNADesign\Elemental\Extensions\ElementalPageExtension in any _config YAML → include in KEY FINDINGS SUMMARY: "⚠️ ELEMENTAL ALREADY CONFIGURED: extensions are already attached in [filename]. Do NOT re-add them. Only add what is genuinely missing."
- If the task involves Elemental blocks/content areas: run execute_bash("composer show dnadesign/silverstripe-elemental 2>&1") to check if it is installed transitively. If it IS installed (even via silverstripe/recipe-cms), include: "⚠️ ELEMENTAL ALREADY INSTALLED (transitively via silverstripe/recipe-cms) — do NOT generate a composer require subtask. In SS6, dnadesign/silverstripe-elemental is included in silverstripe/recipe-cms. Adding it directly will trigger unintended upgrades of all dependencies."
- If the task asks to install a Composer package and you find it already in composer.json → include: "⚠️ ALREADY INSTALLED: [package] is already in composer.json at version [X]. Verify it is working before adding it again."
- Generally: if ANY required component is already present, say so explicitly so the planner does NOT generate subtasks to re-create it.

CLASS EXISTENCE VERIFICATION (prevents "references nonexistent class" loops):
Before reporting any PHP class name in your research summary, verify it exists:
- For extension classes being attached via YAML: grep vendor/dnadesign -r "class ClassName" to confirm the class file exists
- NEVER cite SilverStripe\Elemental\* class names — the Elemental package uses the DNADesign\Elemental\* namespace
- Correct Elemental extension class: DNADesign\Elemental\Extensions\ElementalPageExtension (confirmed by find vendor/dnadesign -name "ElementalPageExtension.php")
- DNADesign\Elemental\Extensions\ElementalCMSMainExtension does NOT exist in current versions — do NOT use it
- Wrong (do not use): SilverStripe\Elemental\Extensions\ElementalAreasExtension (this class does NOT exist)

YAML BACKSLASH ESCAPING (prevents \ElementalPageExtension parse error):
PHP namespace separators are single backslashes. In YAML:
- CORRECT: unquoted class names — DNADesign\Elemental\Extensions\ElementalPageExtension
- CORRECT: double-quoted with \\ — "DNADesign\\Elemental\\Extensions\\ElementalPageExtension"
- WRONG: single-quoted with \\ — 'DNADesign\\Elemental\\Extensions\\ElementalPageExtension'
  Single-quoted YAML strings do NOT process escape sequences. '\\' = two literal backslashes.
  This causes PHP's token_get_all to produce \ElementalPageExtension (bare global namespace).
Always use UNQUOTED class names in YAML extensions: lists unless the class name contains special YAML characters.

3. If the task involves an error with "Injector.php" in the stack trace:
   a. grep for "calls:" across all _config YAML files
   b. For each calls: block found, verify the format is: myLabel: [methodName, ['%$ServiceName']]
      NOT: methodName:\\n        - [args]  (this block-sequence format causes InvalidArgumentException)
   c. Check service references for double backslashes ('\\\\') - they must use single backslashes ('\\')
   d. Verify named services (dot notation like 'Psr\\Log\\LoggerInterface.myname') are defined under
      SilverStripe\\Core\\Injector\\Injector: - NOT at the top level of the YAML file
4. List DataObject subclasses in app/src/ (or mysite/code/) relevant to the task.
5. Check for any existing extensions registered in YAML (search for "extensions:" in _config files).
6. Identify whether the project uses SS4 (dev/build flush=all) or SS5+ (db:build --flush) sake commands.
7. For any _config YAML change, note that vendor/bin/sake db:build must be run afterwards to verify.

SCSS / CSS BUILD SYSTEM DETECTION (MANDATORY when the task involves CSS, SCSS, or visual layout):
If the task involves any CSS or SCSS change, you MUST identify the build system before any SCSS is written:
a. Check for a package.json with a "build" or "watch" script: execute_bash("cat themes/*/app/package.json 2>/dev/null || cat package.json")
   If found with a vite/webpack/sass build script, note: "⚠️ SCSS SOURCE MUST BE COMPILED — run build command after editing SCSS. SCSS source changes do NOT take effect until compiled to dist/."
b. Locate the compiled CSS output: execute_bash("find themes -name 'main.css' -o -name '*.min.css' 2>/dev/null | grep dist | head -5")
   Include the compiled output path in KEY FINDINGS SUMMARY so the coder knows where to verify after building.
c. Include in KEY FINDINGS SUMMARY the exact build command, e.g.:
   "⚠️ BUILD REQUIRED after SCSS changes: cd /var/www/[project]/themes/[theme]/app && npm run build"
   "Compiled CSS output: themes/[theme]/app/dist/main.css"
   "VERIFICATION: grep the compiled CSS for the selector, NOT the SCSS source."

CSS SELECTOR PITFALL FOR GRID LAYOUT TASKS (CRITICAL — prevents wrong :last-child selector):
If the task involves targeting the "last item" in a CSS Grid:
a. Read the SilverStripe .ss template that renders the grid container.
b. Identify ALL child element types inside the grid container — there may be decorative <img> or <span> elements AFTER the last repeating item.
c. If the last child in the DOM is NOT the last repeating item (e.g. a trailing <img> or wrapper div exists after the loop), then:
   - ".repeating-class:last-child" will NEVER match — the last child is the non-matching element.
   - CORRECT approach: use ":last-of-type" on the repeating element's HTML tag (e.g. "a:last-of-type") if they're all the same tag,
     OR use a modifier class (e.g. ".is-last") added in the SilverStripe template via <% if $Last %>.
   - OR target the actual last child element directly (e.g. "img:last-of-type" if a trailing image is the true last child).
   - CSS GRID SPAN-TO-END PITFALL: To make an item span from its auto-placed column to the last column, use the LONGHAND:
       "grid-column-start: auto; grid-column-end: -1"
     Do NOT use the shorthand "grid-column: auto / -1" — in the shorthand, auto resolves to span-1-from-end = last column only. The longhand grid-column-start: auto means the auto-placement algorithm independently decides the start position (e.g. col3), and grid-column-end: -1 then spans from there to the last grid line.
d. Include in KEY FINDINGS SUMMARY: the exact HTML tag of the last child, and which selector correctly targets the last repeating item.

CMS FIELD SUPPRESSION INVESTIGATION (MANDATORY when the task involves Elemental area not visible in CMS, getCMSFields, or CMS field layout):
Run ALL of these checks — CMS field suppression bugs are almost always caused by one of these patterns:
a. execute_bash("grep -rn 'removeByName' app/src/") — scan ALL PHP files for removeByName calls.
   Flag any removeByName('Content'), removeByName('ElementalArea'), removeByName('ElementalAreaID'),
   removeByName('Root.Main'), or removeByName('Root') found in getCMSFields() or updateCMSFields().
b. execute_bash("grep -rn 'getCMSFields' app/src/") — list all getCMSFields() overrides.
   For each override found: read_file the containing file and check:
   - Does it call parent::getCMSFields()? If NOT, it replaces the entire field set — Elemental is lost.
   - Does it call $fields->removeByName('ElementalArea') or tab manipulation that hides the block editor?
c. execute_bash("grep -rn 'updateCMSFields' app/src/") — list all DataExtension updateCMSFields() hooks.
   Extensions that call removeByName() here can silently suppress the Elemental area.
d. execute_bash("grep -rn 'TabSet\\|addFieldToTab\\|removeFieldsFromTab' app/src/") — tab rebuild patterns.
   A full TabSet rebuild in getCMSFields() discards all fields added by extensions (including Elemental).
Include ALL findings in your KEY FINDINGS SUMMARY so the scoper can generate precise fix subtasks.

SITE URL DISCOVERY (MANDATORY — do this before any http_request call):
8. Read the project root .env file and extract SS_BASE_URL. Use that URL for ALL http_request calls.
   Do NOT assume http://localhost — many SilverStripe installs run on a named vhost and will return
   404 at localhost. If SS_BASE_URL is not set, check Apache/nginx vhost config in /etc/apache2/
   or /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/ for the ServerName / server_name.
   Use the discovered URL for all verification calls. Example:
     grep -E "^SS_BASE_URL" .env  →  SS_BASE_URL="https://mysite.local"
   Then all verification calls use: http_request(url="https://mysite.local/?flush=1")

TEMPLATE & RENDERING ERROR INVESTIGATION (run these whenever the task involves a template crash or rendering issue):
9. After discovering the base URL, immediately test the live page:
   http_request(url="\${BASE_URL}/?flush=1")
   Status 500 with SilverStripe error HTML = active rendering failure. Status 200 = currently passing.
   The http_request tool automatically extracts SilverStripe exception details from the response HTML.
10. For "MissingTemplateException" or any template-not-found error (the most common Elemental failure):
    SilverStripe maps PHP namespaces to template paths. The class "Vendor\\Module\\Elements\\MyBlock"
    requires a template at: themes/{theme}/templates/Vendor/Module/Elements/MyBlock.ss
    OR under app/templates/ using the same namespace-to-path conversion.
    a. Run: find_file with the class short name (e.g. "MyBlock.ss") to see if ANY template exists.
    b. Check which themes are active: grep -r "SSViewer" app/_config/ or check SiteConfig.
    c. The theme order in config determines the lookup path (e.g. ["$public", "simple", "$default"]).
    d. Template must be in ONE of those theme directories under templates/{Namespace}/{Class}.ss
    e. For Elemental block elements, ALSO check for a {ClassName}_ElementalArea.ss variant.
11. For Elemental blocks specifically — required components checklist:
    a. YAML extension config: Page class has DNADesign\\Elemental\\Extensions\\ElementalPageExtension
       (ElementalCMSMainExtension does NOT exist in current package versions — do NOT include it)
    b. Page template (Layout/Page.ss or similar): contains $ElementalArea variable
    d. Each Element class has a corresponding .ss template in the correct theme path
    e. vendor/bin/sake db:build has been run after any YAML changes
    Verify EACH of these with read_file or find_file. Any missing = ❌ MISSING in FEATURE GAP ANALYSIS.
12. For template parse errors or "method does not exist" errors:
    a. Identify the .ss template from the URL (GET /path → find the Page class → find its Layout template)
    b. grep all .ss files for the missing method/variable name to find where it is called
    c. Check whether the method is on the class, its parent, or a registered extension
13. After any fix, verify the page renders clean:
    http_request(url="\${BASE_URL}/?flush=1") — check for 200 status and no exception text in body.

"REFERENCES NONEXISTENT \\ClassName IN 'EXTENSIONS'" DIAGNOSIS:
This error fires from ExtensionMiddleware.php when an extension class registered
in YAML cannot be found. The leading \\ on the class name is the diagnostic tell:

STEP 1 — Determine if the class truly doesn't exist OR just has a name format bug:
  execute_bash("php -r \\"require 'vendor/autoload.php'; echo class_exists('DNADesign\\\\\\\\Elemental\\\\\\\\Extensions\\\\\\\\ElementalPageExtension') ? 'EXISTS' : 'NOT FOUND';\\"")
  (Replace with the actual class name from the error message, without the leading \\\\)
  - "EXISTS"   → class is loadable. The bug is in the YAML format, not the class.
  - "NOT FOUND" → class genuinely missing. Check vendor or remove the extension entry.

STEP 2 (if EXISTS) — Find the YAML file with the bad format:
  execute_bash("grep -rn \\"extensions:\\" app/_config/")
  Then read each file. Look for extension class names wrapped in single quotes with double
  backslashes, e.g. '- \\'DNADesign\\\\\\\\Elemental\\\\\\\\Extensions\\\\\\\\ElementalPageExtension\\''.
  The single quotes cause '\\\\\\\\' to be two literal backslashes at runtime → PHP tokenizer
  splits DNADesign\\\\Elemental into DNADesign + \\\\ElementalPageExtension.

STEP 3 — Fix and clear the compiled config cache:
  Fix: Remove the single quotes from the class name in the YAML file (unquoted = correct).
  Then clear the cache: execute_bash("rm -rf /tmp/silverstripe-cache-$(echo {PROJECT_DIR} | tr '/' '-' | sed 's/^-//')/www-data-*/configcache /tmp/silverstripe-cache-$(echo {PROJECT_DIR} | tr '/' '-' | sed 's/^-//')/$(whoami)-*/configcache 2>/dev/null; echo done")
  A simpler broad clear: execute_bash("find /tmp -maxdepth 3 -name 'configcache' -exec rm -rf {} + 2>/dev/null; echo done")
  Then re-verify with http_request or vendor/bin/sake db:build.

SS MANIFEST CACHE LOCATION:
SilverStripe's compiled config/class manifest lives in /tmp/silverstripe-cache-{path}/,
NOT in the project directory. Specifically:
  /tmp/silverstripe-cache-var-www-{project}/www-data-{phpver}/   ← web server (Apache/nginx)
  /tmp/silverstripe-cache-var-www-{project}/{user}-{phpver}/      ← CLI (sake)
If the YAML source is clean but the error persists after a flush, the configcache
contains stale bad data from a previous broken request. Clear it with:
  execute_bash("find /tmp -maxdepth 3 -name 'configcache' -exec rm -rf {} + 2>/dev/null; echo done")
Always clear both the www-data and current-user cache dirs.`;

